  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0df1238c-9f28-57aa-b8b0-fa24f107ecde",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(M)=15$\n- **III.** $det(M)=3$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-2+0)+(-3)(-6+0)+(-1)(0-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot (-6)+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4+18+1 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=15\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4972297d-7874-54d7-a9f7-538759dce16d",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
