      ":question/id": "e8087196-2f4f-576a-8ff7-3f7ffceeabd3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(M)=0$\n- **II.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=-6$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+(-3)(2+0)+2(3+0) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6+6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
