    "question": {
      ":question/id": "239f5a52-9267-5cb1-a7e7-384ec5cafe95",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(T)=15$\n- **II.** $det(T)=0$\n- **III.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)(9-12)+1(0-9)+0 \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =6-9 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=-3\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
