    "question": {
      ":question/id": "70f7f4c0-03ba-5936-a485-e77a37ec57cf",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(S)=2$\n- **II.** $det(S)=0$\n- **III.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-4)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(2-6)+1(-2+2)+(-4)(-6+2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-16+16 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
