  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8459afa9-88ee-58c8-aeec-4803fc157e23",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $R$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(R)=-7$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-4)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(9-2)+(-4)(-3+3)+(-1)(-2+9) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+(-4)\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7-7 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "3ce94f32-1a82-5d9e-b740-746639656326",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
