    "question": {
      ":question/id": "919be3fd-dc71-546a-a0a3-3b80575ee3f0",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(F)=0$\n- **II.** $F$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(F)=-4$\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(9+0)+3(-12+12)+(-3)(0+9) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9+3\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =27-27 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
