    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2f7c82dc-dd6a-56ca-9921-dec1cba67e41",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(F)=9$\n- **II.** $F$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(F)=11$\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3-4)+0+(-2)(-4-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-1+10 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=9\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
