  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "aa0b66bc-3e6a-57ba-8688-2ea916a6aea3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(N)=18$\n- **III.** $det(N)=-3$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(12-6)+2(12-9)+2(-6+9) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6+6+6 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=18\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "6c0afbaa-5084-534c-9c49-89f66b066249",
