    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1cfa4315-cebe-5566-9444-54964abea8b4",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(E)=0$\n- **III.** $det(E)=18$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+(-1)(6-6)+0 \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
