    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0d5f44ce-45dd-5474-ad3c-4202baf1504f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(M)=40$\n- **III.** $det(M)=-1$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-4)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-4-16)+(-4)(-2-4)+(-1)(8-4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-20)+(-4)\\cdot (-6)+(-1)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-20+24-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
