  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "21e24c88-b491-5497-ae3e-82bbe64bd8fd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(Q)=0$\n- **III.** $det(Q)=-24$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-2+6)+1(-6-12)+1(6+4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-18)+1\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =8-18+10 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "46ff6ead-f3d1-5682-bc50-6f73e742af89",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
