  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "be039e29-9887-518a-9cda-1d5be9c202be",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(P)=1$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4)+2(6-6)+(-2)(-2+0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4efcbf5a-d052-58f1-bd8d-3539825e6c28",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
