    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b99d564f-f301-51d4-8ec7-6f04ea1f124c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(T)=14$\n- **II.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=-14$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)(-4+9)+(-1)(-6+0)+2(9+0) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-10+6+18 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=14\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "fc494ccb-9194-592b-a239-af1ad6bc4ad4",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
